# Commit conventions

Releases are automated by **semantic-release** with its default rules, and messages are checked by commitlint
(`@commitlint/config-conventional`). **The commit type decides whether npm gets a new version.**

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <gitmoji> <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

- Subject is imperative and lowercase: `fix(react): 🐛 keep data while revalidating`.
- Header and body lines stay within 100 characters.
- Explain why in the body when the reason is not obvious from the subject.

## Types and release impact

| Type       | Gitmoji | Release   | Use for                                 |
| ---------- | ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `feat`     | 🎸      | **minor** | New capability in a published package   |
| `fix`      | 🐛      | **patch** | Bug fix in a published package          |
| `perf`     | ⚡️      | **patch** | Performance improvement                 |
| `revert`   | ⏪      | **patch** | Reverting an earlier commit             |
| `docs`     | ✏️      | none      | Documentation, READMEs and agent rules  |
| `test`     | 💍      | none      | Tests only                              |
| `refactor` | 💡      | none      | Behavior-preserving restructuring       |
| `style`    | 💄      | none      | Formatting and visual changes           |
| `chore`    | 🤖      | none      | Tooling, dependencies and housekeeping  |
| `ci`       | 🎡      | none      | CI configuration                        |

A `!` after the type or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer publishes a **major** version, whatever the type. Never write that
phrase in a commit body unless the change is breaking.

Releases are repo-wide. One `fix` in any package publishes every package at the same version.

### Choosing the type

- Anything inside `documentation/` is `docs`, even when it fixes a bug in the docs site code.
- Fixing a flaky test is `test`, because users receive nothing new.
- Use `fix` or `feat` only when the published output of a package changes for users.
- A push to `main` also releases any earlier unreleased `feat` or `fix` commits, so a `docs` merge is only release-free
  when nothing is pending. Check before merging:

```bash
git log --no-merges --format='%h %s' "$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 origin/main)..origin/main"
```

## Scopes

Use the package or area: `core`, `react`, `sockets`, `axios`, `graphql`, `firebase`, `firebase-admin`, `cli`, `testing`,
`devtools`, `eslint`, `hyper-flow`, `cache`, `build`, `ci`. Omit the scope when a change spans the repo.

## Issue references

Reference every related issue so it closes when the release is published.

1. Put each issue on its own footer line. `Closes #1, #2` is not parsed reliably.
2. Use `Closes #NNN` when the commit fully resolves the issue, and `Ref #NNN` when it only partly addresses it.
3. Regression test commits use `Ref #NNN`. The fix commit closes the issue.

```
fix(core): 🐛 keep reporting progress for transfers of 100 bytes

The progress calculation treated a completed small transfer as finished before the last event
was emitted.

Closes #363
Closes #433
```
